I can go back and look at it if you really want to know. It's been a 
while since I played with it.

> Looking at the trace-trap in the debuggers stuff I'm beginning to
> think that I made my RUST/SJ implementation more complicated than it
> needed to be. I often seemed to end up with wierd situations with
> trace traps, but perhaps that's because the debugger runs in the same
> CPU mode as the trace interrupt.
>
> Apropos traps, the one trap I can't seem to intercept in RTX is stack
> errors, which unconditionally aborts a task. I guess that makes sense
> for 99.99% of applications.

RTX being your RT emulation under RSX?
Yes, bad stacks can't be handled by programs under RSX.
Both SST and AST vectors assume that the kernel will be able to push 
some data on the user stack before entry. If the user stack is broken, 
it just fails and aborts the task.

> I'd be interested in the RSX-specifics of how the two-program
> implementations work because I guess they represent the O/S lore on
> how to correctly manage memory management of other tasks and how you
> get the mapping information for a separate task. I'm running out of
> space in RTX for the RSX adaption (which was about 4kw) and there are
> some non-essential features that I might be able to implement using
> similiar techniques. I could use PLAS but I can't rely on that since
> it might not have been sysgen'ed into a system.

The way the DECUS debugger does it actually with PLAS. It does a send 
reference call, which both pass a bit of data, and also pass a reference 
to the memory, so that the debugger can inspect and modify memory on its 
own.
However, you can get away without that, and just design a small protocol 
between the two tasks using send and receive calls. It will be slower, 
but perfectly doable.
Thus, in your actual environment, you only have a small stub service 
that every operation call into, and that one in turn sends like an RPC 
to the other task, who do the actual work, and then return a result 
back, for which your stub waits.

But using send reference is much more efficient sometimes...

	Johnny



